			<div class="content" id="post-content-4851307">
									

<p>Porting PM conversation:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>I do have an alternate idea involving an amber mist that transports beings to an oceanic &quot;gateway&quot; world. There, I was thinking of exploring an SCP agent interacting with a cetacean and insectoid, both of whom were also transported to the world. It'd be more of an exploration of power dynamics and territorial advantage between sapient species. Do you think that'd have a better chance than this?</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>It depends. Some reviewers might ask why you're not writing a tale instead, especially if the core anomaly is material that exists in various forms on the site already.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Largely because the SCP in question does lead to a unique world, which requires its own SCP article. Plus, the SCP itself has some unique properties beyond just transporting a person to another place. I was thinking it could also be fairly nutritious, which is a key reason why the SCP agent in the story I plan to tell doesn't die of scurvy or malnutrition after being at sea for so long.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>If it's meant to be primordial, it seems odd that the seaweed would be so conveniently well adapted to a modern human's digestive system. I feel like a different narrative approach would be needed. Timespace oddities and whatnot might even make more sense here from a story standpoint.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Hm. The question is how to do that inside of four hundred words. Would it help if I mentioned it was meant to be in journal format? I know that's been done several times, but… looking over the SCP's, some of my favorite stories have been journal-like narratives, and I think it'd work well in this case.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Could you elaborate a bit? Like, suggesting that this takes place in a past era of Earth? Or that time moves differently in this world, and hunger becomes a non-issue? If it's the latter, I did have an idea about time moving differently on this alien world, so that could inform him returning and noticing that little time has passed.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<hr />
<p>For the new discussion, I feel like the core narrative needs to be better established before you start thinking about mechanics. What is the backstory behind the world's existence and why are random trespassers not immediately ejected? If we know why the world behaves the way it does, we can better advise on how to make the mist effect match.</p>

							</div>

								
					
