														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4852205">
									

<blockquote>
<p>I envisioned it as a &quot;paradise&quot; planet. It's got a warm climate, the seas are freshwater, and underwater food grows readily, even without cultivation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It still seems a little odd that an otherworldly paradise is so well-tailored to human preferences. If you want to make it clear that this is meant to be something not really connected to Earth/humans in origin, you'll need to alter the setup a bit so it's not a human vision of paradise, but an otherworldly individual's vision. If you really want to have the world be catered to humans, there needs to be better contextual explanation for that beyond &quot;it just happens to be like that&quot;.</p>
<p>For a worldbuilding example, you can look at <a href="/scp-1483">SCP-1483</a> (The Third Antarctic Empire) which is effectively set in a parallel universe. There are some similarities to humans, but the inhabitants of the place have different customs and are significantly different physically.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>They were a friendly race, and in an attempt to facilitate trade, sent out these probes in the form of &quot;invitations&quot; to other aquatic worlds. I'm toying with the idea that they were thaumaturgists</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You're going to need to come up with a way to explain how the Foundation would be able to communicate with the race/understand their history. First off, though, decide how human-reliant the paradise setup will be.</p>

