			<div class="content" id="post-content-4852280">
									

<blockquote>
<p>I've gotta disagree about it being tailored to human preferences. For one thing, the world is aquatic.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sure, but it's freshwater not saltwater, and (presumably) has biochemistry that wouldn't kill or sicken a human who came in contact with it. Unless literally everything lives several meters below and nothing ever comes up near the surface and also anything that washes up has no nutritional value whatsoever, a human would probably be alright there compared to, say, a desert or anywhere else that lacks fresh drinking water human bodies can process.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>As for communication… I don't suppose thaumaturgy can cover that? Though I hate the idea of &quot;magic did it&quot; as an answer for everything. Any suggestions?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, it depends on how human-reliant this world is. If there was a connection to Earth at some point, you could have the entities in the other world pull up some ancient texts and try to approximate the most recent language they could have studied. If there was no connection whatsoever to humanity at any point for this world, you're likely going to have to use some sort of advanced convenient translation gimmick like Douglas Adams's Babel fish or just chalk it up to an anomaly.</p>

							</div>

								
					
